 while in the second equation it is $-1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}3}$, then the coefficient of $u$ will turn into $-3$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{3u + 6v = 3; (-u + 5v = 8)\\cdot (3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $u$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3u + 6v &= 3 \\\\\n-3u + 15v &= 24 \\\\\n(6v + 15v) &= (3 + 24) \\\\\n21v &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $u$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$, and add the equations*."}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"}]}
